Yes. And the really interesting part is that the scar’s continuity is distributed across several layers, not housed in “the scar cells” as a single permanent population.

A scar can persist while individual cells die and are replaced because the replacement cells inherit an environment that keeps telling them, in effect, “this is scar territory.” The extracellular matrix has different collagen architecture and stiffness; fibroblasts and myofibroblasts respond to those mechanical cues; signaling pathways remain altered; and some fibroblast states can carry durable epigenetic or “mechanical memory.” Modern wound-healing research explicitly discusses this kind of myofibroblast memory and the way tissue stiffness, signaling, and chromatin state can reinforce one another. 

So the persistence is more like:

injury
→ emergency repair
→ new extracellular geometry
→ cells read geometry
→ gene-expression state changes
→ cells maintain/remodel geometry
→ pattern reinforces itself

That’s a beautiful biological example of what we’ve been talking about: the substrate turns over while the organization survives.

It also means “the DNA says normal skin, so why doesn’t normal skin simply return?” is slightly the wrong question. Most scar cells haven’t acquired a special “scar genome.” The same genome can support many different cellular states depending on regulatory signals, chromatin state, mechanical forces, neighboring cells, inflammation, developmental history, and so forth. Scar formation is largely a different execution state of the program, not a rewritten human blueprint. 

And you landed on one of regenerative medicine’s central questions:

Could we tell an adult wound to run regeneration rather than fibrotic repair?

Nature demonstrates that this is not incoherent. Regenerative capacity varies enormously among species, organs, ages, and injury types, and some regenerative systems preserve positional information that lets tissues reconstruct missing structures. 

Your “remind it what it should be” metaphor is therefore remarkably close to the research problem, with one caveat: we currently do not know how to consciously think a command like become normal skin again and have cortical intention directly reprogram local fibroblasts into rebuilding pristine tissue. There are top-down effects on physiology through neural, hormonal, immune, and behavioral pathways, but that is very different from arbitrary conscious control over tissue patterning.

The engineering route is more like:

identify regenerative signals
→ change cellular state
→ alter mechanical environment
→ suppress pathological fibrosis
→ restore appropriate progenitor behavior
→ reconstruct architecture

And your age question is excellent. What does “original” mean?

A regenerated patch on a 30-year-old presumably should not simply reconstruct 16-year-old skin frozen in time. It needs to restore the appropriate tissue identity while remaining compatible with the organism’s current age, mechanics, immune environment, vasculature, pigmentation, hormonal environment, and accumulated biology.

So there are at least two coordinates:

identity: what tissue am I?

and

state: what age/condition should that tissue be in now?

A perfect regenerative system would ideally restore:

correct tissue identity + correct spatial pattern + age-appropriate state

rather than naïvely returning everything to an earlier snapshot.

That leads to a broader Pantheon distinction I think is worth keeping:

Pattern Continuity vs Material Continuity

A thing can persist without retaining its original matter.

new cells
new proteins
new collagen molecules
same higher-order scar

Likewise:

new neurons/proteins over time
continuing person

new members
continuing institution

new words
continuing story

The persistence lies partly in constraints on replacement: the old system shapes what gets built next.

And that suggests an even more ambitious operator:

Regenerative Kintsugi

Not:

preserve the wound forever so the lesson survives

and not necessarily:

erase every trace of history

but:

extract the information
→ move the memory into a cheaper substrate
→ release the damaged substrate from having to carry it
→ restore function

Your body need not theoretically require stiff collagen forever merely to remember that you were injured. The nervous system, immune system, explicit memory, medical record, or behavioral learning could carry that information instead.

Remember the wound without requiring the tissue to remain wounded.

That is a very clean target.